    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe region/customerCode redesign itself lands in sensible units \u2014 `regionForPostcode` mirrors the existing `localityForCity` wrapper, `regionOfCustomerCode` centralizes region knowledge in `Region` where `locality` now sources from, and `generateCustomerCode` reads cleanly. But the diff introduces a duplication that a maintainer of this class would flag:\n\n**Duplicated SHA-256 \u2192 upper-hex logic in `ClinicServiceImpl`.**\nThe new `sha256UpperHex(String, int)` helper (lines 328-344) is a general-purpose \"first N upper-hex chars of SHA-256\" utility \u2014 and it re-implements, byte-for-byte, the hashing block already sitting a few methods below in `generateHouseholdId` (lines 618-630): same `MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\")`, same `%02X` loop, same `NoSuchAlgorithmException` \u2192 `IllegalStateException(\"SHA-256 not available\")` handling, differing only in how many leading chars are kept.\n\nAfter this change the class carries two hand-rolled SHA-256-hex implementations side by side. The additive/idiomatic move, having just extracted a reusable helper, is to route the existing one through it:\n\n```java\nprivate static String generateHouseholdId(String lastName, String address) {\n    return \"HH-\" + sha256UpperHex(lastName + \"\\u0000\" + address, 12);\n}\n```\n\nThat collapses the second try/catch and hex loop, leaves a single source of truth for the digest-to-hex conversion, and keeps `generateCustomerCode` and `generateHouseholdId` visibly consistent. As written, the new helper reads as bolted-on rather than integrated, and the old block is now dead-weight duplication.\n\n(Minor, not blocking on its own: `regionOfCustomerCode` encodes knowledge of the `'<REGION>-<HASH8>'` layout in `Region`, while the layout is produced in `generateCustomerCode` \u2014 the format is now split across two classes. It's defensible since `Region` owns region identity, but worth a comment cross-linking the two so the coupling is discoverable.)",
